To make this highly readable and keep the cognitive load manageable, I have broken the next expansion down into two punchy sections: Syntax Rules (how sentences are built) and a Worldbuilding Dialogue (seeing the language in action).
1. Syntax Expansion: Building Sentences
Here is how we structure questions, negations, and complex sentences in Vlaems, keeping the "naturalistic irregularity" and medium-heavy French influence in mind.
Questions (V2 Word Order):
Like Standard Dutch, the verb always comes second in main clauses. When asking a yes/no question, the verb jumps to the very front.
Statement: Gij werkt vandaag. (You work today.)
Question: Werkt gij vandaag? (Do you work today?)
Negation (The "Nie" Rule):
As established, nie goes directly after the verb or at the end of the clause for natural flow. For nouns, we use geen (no/none).
Verb negation: Ik wete het nie. (I know it not.)
Noun negation: Ik heb geen geld. (I have no money.)
Relative Clauses (Verb-Kicking):
When you add a descriptive clause (who/which/that), it pushes the verb to the very end of that clause (SOV order). We use the word die for "who/that".
Main clause: De man is mijn vader. (The man is my father.)
Relative clause: die daar loopt. (...who there walks.)
Combined: De man die daar loopt, is mijn vader.
The French Passive Voice:
While Germanic languages use "to become" (worden) for passives, Vlaems frequently borrows the French structure using zijn (to be) + past participle for states of being, especially with French loanwords.
De marché is gesloten. (The market is closed. From French fermé).
2. Worldbuilding in Action: A Coastal Market Dialogue
Let’s see Vlaems in the wild. Here is a short scene between two friends, Bram and Kato, meeting at the coast. Notice the -ke diminutives, the palatal consonants, and the French loanwords (marsjee for market, kafé for cafe, poliesie for police).
