What this scene shows about Vlaems
Maritime French Influence: Words like tampieste (storm) and kapitein show how French dominates specialized, technical, or trade registers, while the core verbs (ruik, wachten, komen) remain solidly Germanic.
West Flemish Prepositions: The shift from naar to noa is a hallmark of the western dialects, giving the language a softer, more rounded vowel sound in common speech.
The Plural "You": The use of gulder clearly distinguishes the captain addressing the whole crew, contrasting with the singular gij or ou used in one-on-one haggling.
Separable Verbs in Imperatives: Haalt ... op! (Pull up!) shows that even in commands, the separable prefix (op) gets kicked to the end of the clause, maintaining strict V2/SOV syntactic rules.
Idiomatic Consistency: Reusing Nie zot (Not crazy) as a blunt refusal shows that colloquial idioms are bleeding into the general lexicon of the language, making it feel like a real, lived-in tongue.
