What this myth shows about Vlaems
1. Diachronic layers

The myth preserves four layers of the language:

Layer	Feature	Example
Archaic Germanic	der "there," nie "nothing," so "so"	Was der nie dan water
Church Latin	in den beginne, verbond	In den beginne was der nie...
French contact	sage, sageman (from sage), kameraad	De Sage van de Eerste Vloed
Modern colloquial	et, nie, gulder	En so is et gebleven
2. Mythological vocabulary

New terms coined or elevated for the myth:

de Eerste Vloed — "the First Flood," the primal sea

de grote weerkeer — "the Great Return," eschatological reunion of all waters

het verbond — "the Covenant" between sea and people

de sage — "the saga/legend," from French sage

sageman — "storyteller," archaic agentive

3. Poetic syntax

Parallelism: De zee geeft, en de zee neemt — biblical-style balance

Anaphora: Zij droomde van... Zij droomde van... — repetitive, hypnotic

Triads: Geen land, geen lucht, geen licht — threefold negation

Inversion: Ut de diepte rees een eiland — verb before subject for elevation

4. Theology of the sea

The myth establishes a covenant theology unique to Vlaems culture:

The sea is maternal (baarde, zoogde) — not hostile, but demanding

The sea is dreaming — reality emerges from her imagination

Humans are children of the sea, not masters of it

Morality is reciprocal: give dead, receive fish

Eschatology is aquatic: all waters become one at the end

This is a coastal animism layered under Catholic surface — exactly the kind of syncretism real Flemish fishing communities preserved.

5. Grammar in mythic register

Pluperfect (had gebaard) for events before the mythic present

Future/imperative (zult, zult) for divine command

Archaic plural gij (before it split into gij singular / gulder plural)

Dative -n preserved only in in den beginne

Existential der instead of modern er

The Vlaems register map (so far)
Register	Scene	Key features
Colloquial	Market	da's, der, nie zot, -ke
Maritime	Docks	tampieste, gesjord, gulder, noa
Liturgical	Funeral	ziele, so zij et, geleiden, Latin
Mythic	Origin	der, nie, so, in den beginne, parallelism

Each register shares the same core grammar but differs in lexicon, morphology, and syntax. This is exactly how real languages stratify.

